1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,680

2
00:00:05,680 --> 00:00:06,370
Hey, everyone.

3
00:00:06,370 --> 00:00:09,460
Thanks for coming to
our CS25 lecture today.

4
00:00:09,460 --> 00:00:12,700
So today we're honored to
have Jim Fan from NVIDIA,

5
00:00:12,700 --> 00:00:14,200
who we're talking
about generalist

6
00:00:14,200 --> 00:00:17,080
agents in open-ended worlds.

7
00:00:17,080 --> 00:00:19,540
And he's a senior AI
research scientist

8
00:00:19,540 --> 00:00:23,650
at NVIDIA, where his mission is
to build generally capable AI

9
00:00:23,650 --> 00:00:27,340
agents with applications to
gaming robotics and software

10
00:00:27,340 --> 00:00:28,820
automation.

11
00:00:28,820 --> 00:00:30,790
And his research spans
foundation models,

12
00:00:30,790 --> 00:00:33,280
multimodal AI,
reinforcement learning,

13
00:00:33,280 --> 00:00:35,900
and open-ended learning.

14
00:00:35,900 --> 00:00:38,500
He obtained his PhD
degree in computer science

15
00:00:38,500 --> 00:00:42,700
from here, at Stanford, advised
by Professor Fei-Fei Li.

16
00:00:42,700 --> 00:00:44,920
And previously, he did
research internships

17
00:00:44,920 --> 00:00:51,130
at OpenAI, Google AI, as well
as Mila-Quebec AI Institute.

18
00:00:51,130 --> 00:00:55,010
So yeah, give it up for Jim.

19
00:00:55,010 --> 00:01:00,120

20
00:01:00,120 --> 00:01:01,950
Yeah, thanks for having me.

21
00:01:01,950 --> 00:01:07,860
So I want to start with
a story of two kittens.

22
00:01:07,860 --> 00:01:12,070
It's a story that gave me a lot
of inspiration over my career.

23
00:01:12,070 --> 00:01:13,950
So I want to share
this one first.

24
00:01:13,950 --> 00:01:17,490
You know, back in 1963,
there are two scientists

25
00:01:17,490 --> 00:01:19,270
from MIT, Held and Hein.

26
00:01:19,270 --> 00:01:21,450
They did this
ingenious experiment

27
00:01:21,450 --> 00:01:25,380
where they put two newborn
kittens in this device,

28
00:01:25,380 --> 00:01:27,720
and the kittens have not
seen the visual world yet.

29
00:01:27,720 --> 00:01:30,270
So it's kind of like
a merry-go-round

30
00:01:30,270 --> 00:01:33,790
where the two kittens are linked
by a rigid mechanical bar.

31
00:01:33,790 --> 00:01:36,330
So their movements are,
like, exactly mirrored.

32
00:01:36,330 --> 00:01:39,300
And there is an active kitten
on the right-hand side,

33
00:01:39,300 --> 00:01:42,600
and that's the only one able to
move freely and then transmit

34
00:01:42,600 --> 00:01:46,470
the motion over this link
to the passive kitten,

35
00:01:46,470 --> 00:01:49,230
which is confined to the basket
and cannot really control its

36
00:01:49,230 --> 00:01:51,450
own movements.

37
00:01:51,450 --> 00:01:52,860
And then, after
a couple of days,

38
00:01:52,860 --> 00:01:55,320
Held and Hein kind of
take the kidneys out

39
00:01:55,320 --> 00:01:59,010
of this merry-go-round and then
did visual testing on them,

40
00:01:59,010 --> 00:02:01,290
and they found that
only the active kitten

41
00:02:01,290 --> 00:02:04,140
was able to develop a
healthy visual motor loop.

42
00:02:04,140 --> 00:02:06,060
Like, you know,
responding correctly

43
00:02:06,060 --> 00:02:08,889
to approaching objects
or, like, visual cliffs.

44
00:02:08,889 --> 00:02:12,850
But the passive kitten did not
have a healthy visual system.

45
00:02:12,850 --> 00:02:15,880
So I find this
experiment fascinating

46
00:02:15,880 --> 00:02:18,580
because it shows the
importance of having

47
00:02:18,580 --> 00:02:21,835
this embodied, active
experience to really go

48
00:02:21,835 --> 00:02:25,970
round a system of intelligence.

49
00:02:25,970 --> 00:02:30,150
And let's put this experiment
in today's AI context, right?

50
00:02:30,150 --> 00:02:32,690
We actually have a very
powerful passive kitten,

51
00:02:32,690 --> 00:02:35,420
and that is ChatGPT.

52
00:02:35,420 --> 00:02:38,150
It passively observes
and rehearses

53
00:02:38,150 --> 00:02:41,610
the text on the internet, and
it doesn't have any embodiment.

54
00:02:41,610 --> 00:02:43,340
And because of
this, its knowledge

55
00:02:43,340 --> 00:02:45,290
is kind of abstract
and ungrounded,

56
00:02:45,290 --> 00:02:47,990
and that partially
contributes to the fact

57
00:02:47,990 --> 00:02:50,510
that ChatGPT
hallucinates things that

58
00:02:50,510 --> 00:02:52,400
are just incompatible
with our common sense

59
00:02:52,400 --> 00:02:55,220
and our physical experience.

60
00:02:55,220 --> 00:02:58,880
And I believe the future
belongs to active kittens, which

61
00:02:58,880 --> 00:03:01,460
translates to generalist agents.

62
00:03:01,460 --> 00:03:05,000
They are the decision-makers
in a constant feedback loop,

63
00:03:05,000 --> 00:03:08,540
and they are embodied in
this fully immersive world.

64
00:03:08,540 --> 00:03:10,430
They are also not
mutually exclusive

65
00:03:10,430 --> 00:03:11,810
with the passive kitten.

66
00:03:11,810 --> 00:03:15,110
And in fact, I see the
active embodiment part

67
00:03:15,110 --> 00:03:18,770
as a layer on top of the
passive pre-training from lots

68
00:03:18,770 --> 00:03:22,150
and lots of internet data.

69
00:03:22,150 --> 00:03:24,790
So, are we there yet?

70
00:03:24,790 --> 00:03:27,130
Have we achieved generalization?

71
00:03:27,130 --> 00:03:29,500
You know, back in
2016, I remember

72
00:03:29,500 --> 00:03:31,570
it was like spring of 2016.

73
00:03:31,570 --> 00:03:34,300
I was sitting an undergraduate
class at Columbia University,

74
00:03:34,300 --> 00:03:36,520
but I wasn't paying
attention to the lecture.

75
00:03:36,520 --> 00:03:40,210
I was watching a board game
tournament on my laptop.

76
00:03:40,210 --> 00:03:43,420
And this screenshot
was the moment

77
00:03:43,420 --> 00:03:47,430
when AlphaGo versus Lee
Sedol-- and AlphaGo won

78
00:03:47,430 --> 00:03:50,190
three matches out of five
and became the first ever

79
00:03:50,190 --> 00:03:52,410
to beat a human champion
at a game of Go.

80
00:03:52,410 --> 00:03:54,690
You know, I remember the
adrenaline that day, right?

81
00:03:54,690 --> 00:03:56,200
I've seen history unfold.

82
00:03:56,200 --> 00:03:57,210
Oh, my god.

83
00:03:57,210 --> 00:03:59,510
We are, you know,
finally getting to HAI.

84
00:03:59,510 --> 00:04:00,970
And everyone is like so excited.

85
00:04:00,970 --> 00:04:03,390
And I think that was the
moment when AI agents entered

86
00:04:03,390 --> 00:04:05,420
the mainstream.

87
00:04:05,420 --> 00:04:09,380
But, you know, like when
the excitement fades,

88
00:04:09,380 --> 00:04:14,570
I felt that even though our
goal was so mighty and so great,

89
00:04:14,570 --> 00:04:17,630
it could only do one
thing and one thing alone.

90
00:04:17,630 --> 00:04:18,330
Right?

91
00:04:18,330 --> 00:04:21,230
And afterwards,
you know, in 2019,

92
00:04:21,230 --> 00:04:23,540
there were more
impressive achievements.

93
00:04:23,540 --> 00:04:26,090
Like OpenAI Five beating
the human champions

94
00:04:26,090 --> 00:04:30,690
at a game of DOTA and AlphaStar
from DeepMind beat StarCraft.

95
00:04:30,690 --> 00:04:33,530
But all of these,
with AlphaGo, they all

96
00:04:33,530 --> 00:04:38,100
have a single kind of theme, and
that is to beat the opponent.

97
00:04:38,100 --> 00:04:41,990
There is this one objective
that the agent needs to do,

98
00:04:41,990 --> 00:04:47,540
and the model is trained on DOTA
or Go cannot generalize to any

99
00:04:47,540 --> 00:04:48,480
other tasks.

100
00:04:48,480 --> 00:04:52,310
It cannot even play other games,
like Super Mario or Minecraft,

101
00:04:52,310 --> 00:04:55,790
and the world is fixed and have
very little room for open-ended

102
00:04:55,790 --> 00:04:59,420
creativity and exploration.

103
00:04:59,420 --> 00:05:01,760
So I argue that a
generalist agent

104
00:05:01,760 --> 00:05:05,190
should have the following
essential properties.

105
00:05:05,190 --> 00:05:06,680
First, it should
be able to pursue

106
00:05:06,680 --> 00:05:10,620
very complex, semantically
rich, and open-ended objectives.

107
00:05:10,620 --> 00:05:13,160
Basically, you explain what
you want in natural language,

108
00:05:13,160 --> 00:05:15,350
and the agent should
perform the actions for you

109
00:05:15,350 --> 00:05:16,760
in a dynamic world.

110
00:05:16,760 --> 00:05:19,670
And second, the agent
should have a large amount

111
00:05:19,670 --> 00:05:22,460
of pre-trained knowledge
instead of knowing

112
00:05:22,460 --> 00:05:27,170
only a few concepts that's
extremely specific to the task.

113
00:05:27,170 --> 00:05:29,020
And third, massively multitask.

114
00:05:29,020 --> 00:05:29,520
Right?

115
00:05:29,520 --> 00:05:32,090
A generalist agent,
as the name implies,

116
00:05:32,090 --> 00:05:35,310
needs to do more than
just a couple of things.

117
00:05:35,310 --> 00:05:39,830
It should be, in the best
case, infinitely multitask,

118
00:05:39,830 --> 00:05:44,120
as expressive as human
language can dictate.

119
00:05:44,120 --> 00:05:45,650
So what does it take?

120
00:05:45,650 --> 00:05:49,310
Correspondingly, we need
three main ingredients.

121
00:05:49,310 --> 00:05:50,950
First is the environment.

122
00:05:50,950 --> 00:05:51,450
Right?

123
00:05:51,450 --> 00:05:54,590
The environment needs
to be open-ended enough

124
00:05:54,590 --> 00:05:58,190
because the agent's
capability is upper bounded

125
00:05:58,190 --> 00:06:00,800
by the environment complexity.

126
00:06:00,800 --> 00:06:03,290
And I'd argue that Earth is
actually a perfect example

127
00:06:03,290 --> 00:06:06,350
because it's so open-ended
in this world we live in

128
00:06:06,350 --> 00:06:08,540
that it allows an
algorithm called

129
00:06:08,540 --> 00:06:12,140
natural evolution to produce all
the diverse forms and behaviors

130
00:06:12,140 --> 00:06:14,000
of life on this planet.

131
00:06:14,000 --> 00:06:17,600
So can we have a simulator that
is essentially a low-fi Earth,

132
00:06:17,600 --> 00:06:23,080
but we can still run
it on the lab clusters?

133
00:06:23,080 --> 00:06:24,820
And second, we need
to provide the agent

134
00:06:24,820 --> 00:06:27,820
with massive pre-training
data because exploration

135
00:06:27,820 --> 00:06:31,480
in an open-ended world from
scratch is just intractable,

136
00:06:31,480 --> 00:06:33,830
and the data will serve
at least two purposes.

137
00:06:33,830 --> 00:06:37,250
One, as a reference manual
on how to do things.

138
00:06:37,250 --> 00:06:39,550
And second, as a
guidance on what

139
00:06:39,550 --> 00:06:42,240
are the interesting
things worth pursuing.

140
00:06:42,240 --> 00:06:46,420
And, you know, GPT is
only at least up to GPT 4.

141
00:06:46,420 --> 00:06:48,470
It only learns from
pure text on the web.

142
00:06:48,470 --> 00:06:51,780
But can we provide agent
with much richer data,

143
00:06:51,780 --> 00:06:57,060
such as video walkthrough, or
like multimedia wiki documents,

144
00:06:57,060 --> 00:07:00,180
and other media forms?

145
00:07:00,180 --> 00:07:04,470
And finally, once we have the
environment and the database,

146
00:07:04,470 --> 00:07:07,860
we are ready to train foundation
models for the agents,

147
00:07:07,860 --> 00:07:11,760
and it should be flexible enough
to pursue the open-ended tasks

148
00:07:11,760 --> 00:07:14,160
without any
task-specific assumptions

149
00:07:14,160 --> 00:07:16,950
and also scalable
enough to compress

150
00:07:16,950 --> 00:07:20,550
all of the multimodal data
that I just described.

151
00:07:20,550 --> 00:07:22,740
And here, language,
I argue, will

152
00:07:22,740 --> 00:07:24,430
play at least two key roles.

153
00:07:24,430 --> 00:07:27,510
One is as a simple and
intuitive interface

154
00:07:27,510 --> 00:07:30,870
to communicate a task, to
communicate the human intentions

155
00:07:30,870 --> 00:07:31,860
to the agent.

156
00:07:31,860 --> 00:07:34,050
And second, as a
bridge to ground

157
00:07:34,050 --> 00:07:37,660
all of the multimodal
concepts and signals.

158
00:07:37,660 --> 00:07:39,940
And that train of
thought landed us

159
00:07:39,940 --> 00:07:45,250
in Minecraft, the best-selling
video game of all time.

160
00:07:45,250 --> 00:07:47,260
And for those who
are unfamiliar,

161
00:07:47,260 --> 00:07:51,350
Minecraft is a procedurally
generated 3D voxel world.

162
00:07:51,350 --> 00:07:52,840
And in the game,
you can basically

163
00:07:52,840 --> 00:07:55,200
do whatever your heart desires.

164
00:07:55,200 --> 00:07:56,700
And what's so special
about the game

165
00:07:56,700 --> 00:08:00,990
is that unlike AlphaGo,
StarCraft, or DOTA,

166
00:08:00,990 --> 00:08:05,280
Minecraft defines no particular
objective to maximize,

167
00:08:05,280 --> 00:08:08,100
no particular opponent
to beat, and doesn't even

168
00:08:08,100 --> 00:08:09,510
have a fixed storyline.

169
00:08:09,510 --> 00:08:12,630
And that makes it very well
suited as a truly open-ended AI

170
00:08:12,630 --> 00:08:13,440
playground.

171
00:08:13,440 --> 00:08:16,980
And here, we see people doing
extremely impressive things

172
00:08:16,980 --> 00:08:17,890
in Minecraft.

173
00:08:17,890 --> 00:08:21,000
Like, this is a
YouTube video where

174
00:08:21,000 --> 00:08:24,570
a gamer built the entire
Hogwarts Castle block by block

175
00:08:24,570 --> 00:08:27,810
by hand in a game.

176
00:08:27,810 --> 00:08:30,300
And here's another example
of someone just digging

177
00:08:30,300 --> 00:08:32,159
a big hole in the
ground and then

178
00:08:32,159 --> 00:08:35,789
making this beautiful
underground temple with a river

179
00:08:35,789 --> 00:08:36,549
nearby.

180
00:08:36,549 --> 00:08:39,799
It's all crafted by hand.

181
00:08:39,799 --> 00:08:40,620
And one more.

182
00:08:40,620 --> 00:08:44,750
This is someone building
a functioning CPU circuit

183
00:08:44,750 --> 00:08:46,730
inside a game because
there is something called

184
00:08:46,730 --> 00:08:51,500
redstone in Minecraft that
you can build circuits out

185
00:08:51,500 --> 00:08:53,090
of it, like logical gates.

186
00:08:53,090 --> 00:08:55,110
And actually the game
is turing complete.

187
00:08:55,110 --> 00:08:58,200
You can simulate a
computer inside a game.

188
00:08:58,200 --> 00:09:00,200
Just think about
how crazy that is.

189
00:09:00,200 --> 00:09:02,720
And here I want to
highlight a number, that

190
00:09:02,720 --> 00:09:06,060
is 140 million active players.

191
00:09:06,060 --> 00:09:08,690
And just to put this
number in perspective,

192
00:09:08,690 --> 00:09:12,950
this is more than twice
the population of UK.

193
00:09:12,950 --> 00:09:14,870
And that is the amount
of people playing

194
00:09:14,870 --> 00:09:17,315
Minecraft on a daily basis.

195
00:09:17,315 --> 00:09:18,690
And, you know, it
just so happens

196
00:09:18,690 --> 00:09:22,350
that gamers are generally
happier than PhDs.

197
00:09:22,350 --> 00:09:26,350
So they love to stream and
share what they're doing,

198
00:09:26,350 --> 00:09:30,220
and that produces a huge amount
of data every day online.

199
00:09:30,220 --> 00:09:33,840
And there's this treasure
trove of learning materials

200
00:09:33,840 --> 00:09:36,880
that we can tap into for
training generalist agents.

201
00:09:36,880 --> 00:09:41,270
You know, remember, the data is
the key for foundation models.

202
00:09:41,270 --> 00:09:45,740
So we introduce MineDojo,
a new open framework

203
00:09:45,740 --> 00:09:49,760
to help the community develop
generally capable agents using

204
00:09:49,760 --> 00:09:51,805
Minecraft as a kind
of primordial soup.

205
00:09:51,805 --> 00:09:55,070

206
00:09:55,070 --> 00:09:57,140
MineDojo features
three major parts--

207
00:09:57,140 --> 00:10:00,920
an open-ended environment, an
internet-scale knowledge base,

208
00:10:00,920 --> 00:10:04,490
and then a generalist agent
developed with a simulator

209
00:10:04,490 --> 00:10:06,200
and a massive data.

210
00:10:06,200 --> 00:10:08,600
So let's zoom in the first one.

211
00:10:08,600 --> 00:10:11,390
Here is a sample gallery
of the interesting things

212
00:10:11,390 --> 00:10:14,870
that you can do
with MineDojo's API.

213
00:10:14,870 --> 00:10:17,600
We feature a massive
benchmarking suite of more than

214
00:10:17,600 --> 00:10:22,610
3,000 tasks, and this is by far
the largest open source agent

215
00:10:22,610 --> 00:10:25,130
benchmark to our knowledge.

216
00:10:25,130 --> 00:10:27,320
And we implement a
very versatile API

217
00:10:27,320 --> 00:10:29,400
that unlocks the full
potential of the game.

218
00:10:29,400 --> 00:10:33,800
Like, for example, MineDojo
supports multimodal observation

219
00:10:33,800 --> 00:10:37,040
and for action
space, like moving,

220
00:10:37,040 --> 00:10:39,380
or attack, or
inventory management.

221
00:10:39,380 --> 00:10:42,140
And then it can be
customized at every detail.

222
00:10:42,140 --> 00:10:47,510
Like you can tweak the terrains,
the weather, block placement,

223
00:10:47,510 --> 00:10:49,400
monster spawning,
and just anything

224
00:10:49,400 --> 00:10:52,390
you want to customize
in the game.

225
00:10:52,390 --> 00:10:55,900
And given the simulator,
we introduce around 1,500

226
00:10:55,900 --> 00:10:59,740
programmatic tasks, which are
tasks that have grown through

227
00:10:59,740 --> 00:11:02,920
success conditions
defined in Python code.

228
00:11:02,920 --> 00:11:04,570
And you can also
explicitly write down

229
00:11:04,570 --> 00:11:07,900
like the sparse or the dense
reward functions using this API.

230
00:11:07,900 --> 00:11:10,870
And some examples are like
harvesting different resources,

231
00:11:10,870 --> 00:11:14,830
unlocking the tech tree, or
fighting various monsters

232
00:11:14,830 --> 00:11:16,510
and getting reward.

233
00:11:16,510 --> 00:11:18,250
And all these tasks
come with language

234
00:11:18,250 --> 00:11:21,210
prompts that are templated.

235
00:11:21,210 --> 00:11:24,990
Next, we also introduce
1,500 creative tasks that are

236
00:11:24,990 --> 00:11:27,510
free-form and open-ended,
and that is in contrast

237
00:11:27,510 --> 00:11:30,380
to the programmatic
tasks I just mentioned.

238
00:11:30,380 --> 00:11:35,040
So, for example, let's say we
want the agent to put a house.

239
00:11:35,040 --> 00:11:37,320
But what makes a
house a house, right?

240
00:11:37,320 --> 00:11:39,030
It is ill-defined.

241
00:11:39,030 --> 00:11:40,520
And just like image
generation, you

242
00:11:40,520 --> 00:11:44,960
don't know if it generates
a cat correctly or not.

243
00:11:44,960 --> 00:11:47,450
So it's very difficult to
use simple Python programs

244
00:11:47,450 --> 00:11:50,420
to give these kind of
task reward functions.

245
00:11:50,420 --> 00:11:54,170
And the best way is to use
foundation models trained

246
00:11:54,170 --> 00:11:57,800
on internet-scale knowledge
so that the model itself

247
00:11:57,800 --> 00:12:01,220
understands abstract
concepts like, you know,

248
00:12:01,220 --> 00:12:04,140
the concept of a house.

249
00:12:04,140 --> 00:12:05,700
And finally, there's
one task that

250
00:12:05,700 --> 00:12:07,980
holds a very special
status called playthrough,

251
00:12:07,980 --> 00:12:12,210
which is to beat the final boss
of Minecraft, the Ender Dragon.

252
00:12:12,210 --> 00:12:14,560
So Minecraft doesn't
force you to do this task.

253
00:12:14,560 --> 00:12:16,830
As we said, it doesn't
have a fixed storyline,

254
00:12:16,830 --> 00:12:19,350
but it's still considered
a really big milestone

255
00:12:19,350 --> 00:12:22,920
for any kind of
beginner human players.

256
00:12:22,920 --> 00:12:25,860
I want to highlight it is an
extremely difficult task that

257
00:12:25,860 --> 00:12:29,040
requires very complex
preparation, exploration,

258
00:12:29,040 --> 00:12:30,730
and also martial skills.

259
00:12:30,730 --> 00:12:32,610
And for an average
human, it will

260
00:12:32,610 --> 00:12:36,750
take many hours or
even days to solve

261
00:12:36,750 --> 00:12:38,610
easily over like
one million action

262
00:12:38,610 --> 00:12:40,300
steps in a single episode.

263
00:12:40,300 --> 00:12:43,650
And that would be the longest
benchmarking task for policy

264
00:12:43,650 --> 00:12:45,270
learning ever created here.

265
00:12:45,270 --> 00:12:49,170
So I admit I am personally
a below-average human.

266
00:12:49,170 --> 00:12:51,930
I was never able to
beat Ender Dragon,

267
00:12:51,930 --> 00:12:53,250
and my friends laugh at me.

268
00:12:53,250 --> 00:12:58,660
And I'm like, OK, one day, my
AI will avenge my poor skills.

269
00:12:58,660 --> 00:12:59,160
Right?

270
00:12:59,160 --> 00:13:03,050
That was one of the
motivations for this project.

271
00:13:03,050 --> 00:13:05,850
Now, let's move on to
the second ingredient,

272
00:13:05,850 --> 00:13:09,050
the internet-scale knowledge
base part of MineDojo.

273
00:13:09,050 --> 00:13:12,980
We offer three data sets here,
the YouTube, Wiki, and Reddit.

274
00:13:12,980 --> 00:13:16,370
And combined, they are the
largest open-ended agent

275
00:13:16,370 --> 00:13:20,590
behavior database ever
compiled to our knowledge.

276
00:13:20,590 --> 00:13:23,560
The first is YouTube,
and we already

277
00:13:23,560 --> 00:13:27,430
said Minecraft is one of the
most streamed games on YouTube.

278
00:13:27,430 --> 00:13:30,370
And gamers love to narrate
what they're doing.

279
00:13:30,370 --> 00:13:34,780
So we collected more than
700,000 videos with two billion

280
00:13:34,780 --> 00:13:37,390
words in the
corresponding transcripts.

281
00:13:37,390 --> 00:13:40,000
And these transcripts
will help the agent

282
00:13:40,000 --> 00:13:44,560
learn about human strategies and
creativities without us manually

283
00:13:44,560 --> 00:13:45,420
labeling things.

284
00:13:45,420 --> 00:13:48,100

285
00:13:48,100 --> 00:13:50,470
And second, the
Minecraft player base

286
00:13:50,470 --> 00:13:53,080
is so crazy that
they have compiled

287
00:13:53,080 --> 00:13:57,370
a huge Minecraft-specific
Wikipedia that basically

288
00:13:57,370 --> 00:14:00,520
explains everything
you ever need to know

289
00:14:00,520 --> 00:14:02,210
in every version of the game.

290
00:14:02,210 --> 00:14:03,260
It's crazy.

291
00:14:03,260 --> 00:14:07,870
And we scrape 7,000 wiki pages
with interleaving multimodal

292
00:14:07,870 --> 00:14:10,420
data like images,
tables, and diagrams,

293
00:14:10,420 --> 00:14:12,340
and here are some screenshots.

294
00:14:12,340 --> 00:14:15,100
Like this is a gallery
of all of the monsters

295
00:14:15,100 --> 00:14:17,410
and their corresponding
behaviors,

296
00:14:17,410 --> 00:14:19,870
like spawn and attack patterns.

297
00:14:19,870 --> 00:14:22,420
And also, like the
thousands of crafting

298
00:14:22,420 --> 00:14:26,080
recipes are all present on the
Wiki, and we scrape all of them.

299
00:14:26,080 --> 00:14:28,360
And while like complex
diagrams, and tables,

300
00:14:28,360 --> 00:14:32,140
and embedded figures, now we
have something like GPT-4V.

301
00:14:32,140 --> 00:14:36,680
It may be able to understand
many of these diagrams.

302
00:14:36,680 --> 00:14:39,170
And finally, the
Minecraft subreddit

303
00:14:39,170 --> 00:14:43,610
is one of the most active
forums across the entire Reddit.

304
00:14:43,610 --> 00:14:45,500
And players showcase
their creations

305
00:14:45,500 --> 00:14:47,510
and also ask questions for help.

306
00:14:47,510 --> 00:14:52,220
So we scraped more than 300,000
posts from the Minecraft Reddit.

307
00:14:52,220 --> 00:14:54,230
And here are some
examples of how

308
00:14:54,230 --> 00:14:59,180
people use the Reddit as a kind
of Stack Overflow for Minecraft.

309
00:14:59,180 --> 00:15:01,280
And we can see that some
of the top-voted answers

310
00:15:01,280 --> 00:15:02,630
are actually quite good.

311
00:15:02,630 --> 00:15:06,033
Like someone is asking, oh,
why doesn't my wheat farm grow?

312
00:15:06,033 --> 00:15:07,700
And the answer says
you need to light up

313
00:15:07,700 --> 00:15:09,240
the room with more torches.

314
00:15:09,240 --> 00:15:12,270
You don't have enough lighting.

315
00:15:12,270 --> 00:15:15,870
Now, given the massive task
suite and internet data,

316
00:15:15,870 --> 00:15:21,180
we have the essential components
to build generalist agents.

317
00:15:21,180 --> 00:15:23,580
So in the first
MineDojo paper, we

318
00:15:23,580 --> 00:15:25,840
introduced a foundation
model called MineCLIP.

319
00:15:25,840 --> 00:15:27,850
And the idea is very simple.

320
00:15:27,850 --> 00:15:30,220
I can explain it
in three slides.

321
00:15:30,220 --> 00:15:31,870
Basically, for our
YouTube database,

322
00:15:31,870 --> 00:15:35,500
we have time-aligned
videos and transcripts.

323
00:15:35,500 --> 00:15:38,230
And these are actually
the real tutorial videos

324
00:15:38,230 --> 00:15:39,620
from our data set.

325
00:15:39,620 --> 00:15:42,400
You see on the third
clip, you know,

326
00:15:42,400 --> 00:15:44,200
as I raise my ax in
front of this pig,

327
00:15:44,200 --> 00:15:47,590
there's only one thing that
you know going to happen.

328
00:15:47,590 --> 00:15:49,780
There is actually
someone who said this,

329
00:15:49,780 --> 00:15:52,890
a big YouTuber of Minecraft.

330
00:15:52,890 --> 00:15:55,800
And then, given
this data, we train

331
00:15:55,800 --> 00:15:58,650
MineCLIP in the same
spirit as OpenAI CLIP.

332
00:15:58,650 --> 00:16:00,930
So for those who are
unfamiliar, OpenAI CLIP

333
00:16:00,930 --> 00:16:04,620
is a contrastive model
that learns the association

334
00:16:04,620 --> 00:16:06,580
between image and its caption.

335
00:16:06,580 --> 00:16:08,800
And here it's a
very similar idea.

336
00:16:08,800 --> 00:16:12,240
But this time it is a video
text contrastive model,

337
00:16:12,240 --> 00:16:17,130
and we associate the text
with a video snippet that runs

338
00:16:17,130 --> 00:16:19,010
about 8 to 16 seconds each.

339
00:16:19,010 --> 00:16:22,630

340
00:16:22,630 --> 00:16:25,720
And intuitively, MineCLIP
learns the association

341
00:16:25,720 --> 00:16:27,370
between the video
and the transcript

342
00:16:27,370 --> 00:16:30,850
that describes the
activity in the video.

343
00:16:30,850 --> 00:16:33,610
And MineCLIP outputs a
score between 0 and 1,

344
00:16:33,610 --> 00:16:36,280
where one means a perfect
correlation between the text

345
00:16:36,280 --> 00:16:41,020
and video, and 0 means the text
is irrelevant to the activity.

346
00:16:41,020 --> 00:16:45,370
So you see, this is effectively
a language-prompted foundation

347
00:16:45,370 --> 00:16:49,000
reward model that knows
the nuances of things

348
00:16:49,000 --> 00:16:54,730
like forests, animal, behaviors,
and architectures in Minecraft.

349
00:16:54,730 --> 00:16:57,250
So how do we use
MineCLIP in action?

350
00:16:57,250 --> 00:17:01,220
Here's an example of our agent
interacting with the simulator.

351
00:17:01,220 --> 00:17:04,930
And here, the task is
shear sheep to obtain wool.

352
00:17:04,930 --> 00:17:08,230
And as the agent explores
in the simulator,

353
00:17:08,230 --> 00:17:12,760
it generates a video snippet
as a moving window, which

354
00:17:12,760 --> 00:17:15,310
can be encoded and
fed into MineCLIP

355
00:17:15,310 --> 00:17:19,065
along with an encoding
of the text prompt here.

356
00:17:19,065 --> 00:17:20,910
And MineCLIP completes
the association.

357
00:17:20,910 --> 00:17:21,410
Right?

358
00:17:21,410 --> 00:17:24,440
The higher the
association is, the more

359
00:17:24,440 --> 00:17:27,230
the agent's behavior
in this video aligns

360
00:17:27,230 --> 00:17:30,710
with the language, which is
a task you want it to do.

361
00:17:30,710 --> 00:17:32,270
And that becomes
the reward function

362
00:17:32,270 --> 00:17:35,390
to any reinforcement
learning algorithm.

363
00:17:35,390 --> 00:17:38,240
So this looks very
familiar, right?

364
00:17:38,240 --> 00:17:42,050
Because It's essentially
RL from human feedback,

365
00:17:42,050 --> 00:17:45,970
or RLHF in Minecraft.

366
00:17:45,970 --> 00:17:48,370
And RLHF was the
cornerstone algorithm

367
00:17:48,370 --> 00:17:50,710
that made ChatGPT
possible, and I

368
00:17:50,710 --> 00:17:53,440
believe it will play a critical
role in generalist agents

369
00:17:53,440 --> 00:17:55,940
as well.

370
00:17:55,940 --> 00:17:58,730
I'll quickly gloss over
some quantitative results.

371
00:17:58,730 --> 00:18:02,480
I promise there won't be like
many tables of numbers here.

372
00:18:02,480 --> 00:18:05,210
For these eight tasks, we show
the percentage success rate

373
00:18:05,210 --> 00:18:07,760
over 200 test episodes.

374
00:18:07,760 --> 00:18:10,670
And here in the green
circle is two variants

375
00:18:10,670 --> 00:18:12,160
of our MineCLIP method.

376
00:18:12,160 --> 00:18:15,640
And in the orange circles
are the baselines.

377
00:18:15,640 --> 00:18:17,890
So I'll highlight
one baseline, which

378
00:18:17,890 --> 00:18:21,670
is that we construct a dense
reward function manually

379
00:18:21,670 --> 00:18:24,550
for each task using
the MineDojo API.

380
00:18:24,550 --> 00:18:25,930
It's a Python API.

381
00:18:25,930 --> 00:18:29,332
And you can consider this
column as a kind of Oracle,

382
00:18:29,332 --> 00:18:30,790
the upper bound of
the performance,

383
00:18:30,790 --> 00:18:32,500
because we put a
lot of human efforts

384
00:18:32,500 --> 00:18:37,000
into designing these reward
functions just for the tasks.

385
00:18:37,000 --> 00:18:40,600
And we can see that
MineCLIP is able to match

386
00:18:40,600 --> 00:18:42,140
the quality of many of these.

387
00:18:42,140 --> 00:18:44,200
Not all of them, but
many of these manual

388
00:18:44,200 --> 00:18:45,580
engineering rewards.

389
00:18:45,580 --> 00:18:47,770
It is important to
highlight that MineCLIP

390
00:18:47,770 --> 00:18:49,240
is open vocabulary.

391
00:18:49,240 --> 00:18:52,210
So we use a single model for
all of these tasks instead

392
00:18:52,210 --> 00:18:55,900
of one model for each, and we
simply prompt the reward model

393
00:18:55,900 --> 00:18:58,100
with different tasks.

394
00:18:58,100 --> 00:18:59,780
And that's the only variation.

395
00:18:59,780 --> 00:19:03,000

396
00:19:03,000 --> 00:19:04,740
One major feature
of foundation model

397
00:19:04,740 --> 00:19:06,940
is strong generalization
out of box.

398
00:19:06,940 --> 00:19:10,860
So can now agent generalize
to dramatic changes

399
00:19:10,860 --> 00:19:12,300
in the visual appearance?

400
00:19:12,300 --> 00:19:15,420
So we did this experiment
where, during training, we only

401
00:19:15,420 --> 00:19:21,420
train our agents on a default
terrain at noon on a sunny day.

402
00:19:21,420 --> 00:19:25,320
But we tested zero-shot in
a diverse range of terrains,

403
00:19:25,320 --> 00:19:28,120
weathers, and day-night cycles,
and you can customize everything

404
00:19:28,120 --> 00:19:29,100
in MineDojo.

405
00:19:29,100 --> 00:19:30,690
And in our paper,
we have numbers

406
00:19:30,690 --> 00:19:33,000
showing that MineCLIP
significantly

407
00:19:33,000 --> 00:19:35,670
beats an off-the-shelf
visual encoder when

408
00:19:35,670 --> 00:19:38,580
facing these kind of
distribution shift out of box.

409
00:19:38,580 --> 00:19:40,125
And this is no surprise, right?

410
00:19:40,125 --> 00:19:42,720
Because MineCLIP was trained
on hundreds of thousands

411
00:19:42,720 --> 00:19:46,990
of clips from Minecraft
videos on YouTube,

412
00:19:46,990 --> 00:19:51,140
which have a very good
coverage of all the scenarios.

413
00:19:51,140 --> 00:19:55,850
And I think that is just a
testament to the big advantage

414
00:19:55,850 --> 00:19:59,750
of using internet-scale data
because you get robustness out

415
00:19:59,750 --> 00:20:01,450
of box.

416
00:20:01,450 --> 00:20:04,000
And here are some demos of
our learned agent behaviors

417
00:20:04,000 --> 00:20:05,710
on various tasks.

418
00:20:05,710 --> 00:20:08,770
So you may notice that these
tasks are relatively short,

419
00:20:08,770 --> 00:20:12,010
around like 100
to 500 timestamps.

420
00:20:12,010 --> 00:20:14,320
And that is because
MineCLIP is not

421
00:20:14,320 --> 00:20:18,200
able to plan over very
long time horizons.

422
00:20:18,200 --> 00:20:22,120
And it is an inherent limitation
in the training pipeline

423
00:20:22,120 --> 00:20:25,370
because we could only use 8
to 16 seconds of the video,

424
00:20:25,370 --> 00:20:28,330
so it's constrained
to like short actions.

425
00:20:28,330 --> 00:20:31,810
But our hope is to build an
agent that can explore and make

426
00:20:31,810 --> 00:20:34,730
new discoveries autonomously
just all by itself,

427
00:20:34,730 --> 00:20:35,950
and it keeps going.

428
00:20:35,950 --> 00:20:39,860
And in 2022, this goal seems
quite out of reach for us.

429
00:20:39,860 --> 00:20:43,070
MineDojo was June 2022.

430
00:20:43,070 --> 00:20:44,760
And this year,
something happened.

431
00:20:44,760 --> 00:20:49,710
And that is GPT-4, a language
model that's so good at coding

432
00:20:49,710 --> 00:20:53,820
and long-horizon planning, so
we just cannot sit still, right?

433
00:20:53,820 --> 00:20:57,120
We built Voyager, the
first large language

434
00:20:57,120 --> 00:21:00,150
model-powered lifelong
learning agent.

435
00:21:00,150 --> 00:21:02,790
And when we set Voyager
loose in Minecraft,

436
00:21:02,790 --> 00:21:04,690
we see that it just keeps going.

437
00:21:04,690 --> 00:21:07,500
And by the way, all
these video snippets

438
00:21:07,500 --> 00:21:09,810
are from a single
episode of Voyager.

439
00:21:09,810 --> 00:21:11,950
It's not from
different episodes.

440
00:21:11,950 --> 00:21:13,550
It's a single one.

441
00:21:13,550 --> 00:21:17,030
And we see that Voyager is
just able to keep exploring

442
00:21:17,030 --> 00:21:21,080
the terrains, mine all kinds of
materials, fight monsters, craft

443
00:21:21,080 --> 00:21:24,530
hundreds of recipes, and
unlock an ever-expanding tree

444
00:21:24,530 --> 00:21:26,750
of diverse skills.

445
00:21:26,750 --> 00:21:30,020
So how do we do this?

446
00:21:30,020 --> 00:21:32,450
If we want to use the
full power of GPT-4,

447
00:21:32,450 --> 00:21:35,150
a central question is
how to stringify things,

448
00:21:35,150 --> 00:21:40,100
converting this 3D world into
a textual representation.

449
00:21:40,100 --> 00:21:42,520
We need a magic box here.

450
00:21:42,520 --> 00:21:46,210
And thankfully, again, the crazy
Minecraft community already

451
00:21:46,210 --> 00:21:49,390
built one for us, and it's
been around for many years.

452
00:21:49,390 --> 00:21:52,090
It's called Mineflayer,
a high-level JavaScript

453
00:21:52,090 --> 00:21:55,810
API that's actively maintained
to work with any Minecraft

454
00:21:55,810 --> 00:21:56,920
version.

455
00:21:56,920 --> 00:21:58,570
And the beauty of
Mineflayer is it

456
00:21:58,570 --> 00:22:02,500
has access to the game
state's surrounding agent,

457
00:22:02,500 --> 00:22:05,740
like the nearby blocks,
animals, and enemies.

458
00:22:05,740 --> 00:22:09,190
So we effectively have a
ground-truth perception module

459
00:22:09,190 --> 00:22:10,490
as textual input.

460
00:22:10,490 --> 00:22:14,410
And at the same time, Mineflayer
also supports action APIs

461
00:22:14,410 --> 00:22:18,340
that we can compose skills.

462
00:22:18,340 --> 00:22:20,470
And now that we can
convert everything to text,

463
00:22:20,470 --> 00:22:24,220
we are ready to construct
an agent on top of GPT-4.

464
00:22:24,220 --> 00:22:26,710
So on a high level, there
are three components.

465
00:22:26,710 --> 00:22:30,580
One is a coding module
that writes JavaScript code

466
00:22:30,580 --> 00:22:33,040
to control the
game bot, and it's

467
00:22:33,040 --> 00:22:35,950
the main module that generates
the executable actions.

468
00:22:35,950 --> 00:22:40,150
And second, we have a code base
to store the correctly written

469
00:22:40,150 --> 00:22:42,250
code and look it
up in the future

470
00:22:42,250 --> 00:22:44,440
if the agent needs
to recall the skill.

471
00:22:44,440 --> 00:22:46,708
And in this way, we
don't duplicate efforts.

472
00:22:46,708 --> 00:22:49,000
And when we're facing similar
situations in the future,

473
00:22:49,000 --> 00:22:50,830
the agent knows what to do.

474
00:22:50,830 --> 00:22:54,130
And third, we have
a curriculum that

475
00:22:54,130 --> 00:22:58,150
proposes what to do next, given
the agent's current capabilities

476
00:22:58,150 --> 00:22:59,830
and also situation.

477
00:22:59,830 --> 00:23:03,940
And when you wire these
components up together,

478
00:23:03,940 --> 00:23:07,240
you get a loop that drives the
agent indefinitely and achieves

479
00:23:07,240 --> 00:23:09,950
something like
lifelong learning.

480
00:23:09,950 --> 00:23:13,160
So let's zoom in
the center module.

481
00:23:13,160 --> 00:23:16,850
We prompt GPT-4 with
documentations and examples

482
00:23:16,850 --> 00:23:19,730
on how to use a subset
of the Mineflayer API,

483
00:23:19,730 --> 00:23:22,850
and GPT-4 writes code
to take actions given

484
00:23:22,850 --> 00:23:24,710
the current assigned task.

485
00:23:24,710 --> 00:23:27,060
And because JavaScript
runs a code interpreter,

486
00:23:27,060 --> 00:23:29,630
GPT-4 is able to define
functions on the fly

487
00:23:29,630 --> 00:23:32,030
and run it interactively.

488
00:23:32,030 --> 00:23:34,740
But the code that GPT-4 writes
isn't always correct, right?

489
00:23:34,740 --> 00:23:36,740
Just like human errors,
you can't get everything

490
00:23:36,740 --> 00:23:38,060
correct on the first try.

491
00:23:38,060 --> 00:23:41,000
So we developed an iterative
prompting mechanism

492
00:23:41,000 --> 00:23:42,890
to refine the program.

493
00:23:42,890 --> 00:23:45,540
And there are three
types of feedback here.

494
00:23:45,540 --> 00:23:46,655
The environment feedback.

495
00:23:46,655 --> 00:23:48,740
Like, you know, what
are the new materials

496
00:23:48,740 --> 00:23:51,200
you got after taking
an action, or, you

497
00:23:51,200 --> 00:23:52,800
know, some enemies nearby?

498
00:23:52,800 --> 00:23:55,740
And the execution error from
the JavaScript interpreter,

499
00:23:55,740 --> 00:23:58,970
if you wrote some buggy code
like undefined variable,

500
00:23:58,970 --> 00:24:01,820
for example, if it
hallucinates something.

501
00:24:01,820 --> 00:24:07,010
And another GPT-4 that provides
critique through self-reflection

502
00:24:07,010 --> 00:24:10,730
from the Asian state and the
world state, and that also helps

503
00:24:10,730 --> 00:24:13,310
refine the program effectively.

504
00:24:13,310 --> 00:24:14,900
So I want to show
some quick example

505
00:24:14,900 --> 00:24:18,620
of how the critic provides
feedback on the task completion

506
00:24:18,620 --> 00:24:19,610
progress.

507
00:24:19,610 --> 00:24:21,170
So let's say in
the first example,

508
00:24:21,170 --> 00:24:24,620
the task is to craft
a spyglass, and GPT-4

509
00:24:24,620 --> 00:24:27,320
looks at the agent's
inventory and decides

510
00:24:27,320 --> 00:24:30,470
that it has enough copper
but not enough amherst

511
00:24:30,470 --> 00:24:32,330
as a material.

512
00:24:32,330 --> 00:24:35,630
And the second task is to kill
three sheeps to collect food.

513
00:24:35,630 --> 00:24:38,340
And each sheep drops
one unit of wool,

514
00:24:38,340 --> 00:24:40,410
but there are only two
units in the inventory.

515
00:24:40,410 --> 00:24:43,490
So two different reasons and
says that, OK, you have one more

516
00:24:43,490 --> 00:24:46,910
sheep to go, and likewise.

517
00:24:46,910 --> 00:24:49,770
Now, moving on to
the second part.

518
00:24:49,770 --> 00:24:52,470
Once Voyager implements
a skill correctly,

519
00:24:52,470 --> 00:24:55,350
we save it to our
persistent storage.

520
00:24:55,350 --> 00:24:58,980
And you can think of the skill
library as a code repository

521
00:24:58,980 --> 00:25:02,640
written entirely by a language
model through interaction with

522
00:25:02,640 --> 00:25:04,140
the 3D world.

523
00:25:04,140 --> 00:25:07,020
And the agent can
record new skills

524
00:25:07,020 --> 00:25:09,570
and also retrieve skills
from the library facing

525
00:25:09,570 --> 00:25:11,727
similar situations
in the future,

526
00:25:11,727 --> 00:25:14,310
so it doesn't have to go through
this whole program refinement

527
00:25:14,310 --> 00:25:16,870
that we just saw, which
is quite inefficient.

528
00:25:16,870 --> 00:25:19,950
But you do it once,
you save it to disk.

529
00:25:19,950 --> 00:25:24,240
And in this way, Voyager kind of
bootstraps its own capabilities

530
00:25:24,240 --> 00:25:29,473
recursively as it explores
and experiments in a game.

531
00:25:29,473 --> 00:25:30,890
And let's dive a
little bit deeper

532
00:25:30,890 --> 00:25:33,440
into how the skill
library is implemented.

533
00:25:33,440 --> 00:25:35,940
So this is how we
insert a new skill.

534
00:25:35,940 --> 00:25:39,410
First, we use GPT 3.5
to summarize the program

535
00:25:39,410 --> 00:25:42,140
into plain English, and
summarization is very easy.

536
00:25:42,140 --> 00:25:44,540
And GPT-4 is
expensive, so we just

537
00:25:44,540 --> 00:25:47,330
go for a cheaper, cheaper tier.

538
00:25:47,330 --> 00:25:50,990
And then we embed this
summary as the key,

539
00:25:50,990 --> 00:25:53,600
and we save the program,
which is a bunch of code,

540
00:25:53,600 --> 00:25:54,810
as the value.

541
00:25:54,810 --> 00:25:57,200
And we find that doing
this makes retrieval

542
00:25:57,200 --> 00:25:59,720
better because the
summary is more semantic

543
00:25:59,720 --> 00:26:02,030
and the code is a
bit more discreet.

544
00:26:02,030 --> 00:26:03,570
And you insert it.

545
00:26:03,570 --> 00:26:06,600

546
00:26:06,600 --> 00:26:09,160
And now for the
retrieval process.

547
00:26:09,160 --> 00:26:10,980
When Voyager is faced
with a new task,

548
00:26:10,980 --> 00:26:13,680
let's say craft iron
pickaxe, we again

549
00:26:13,680 --> 00:26:17,760
use GPT 3.5 to generate a
hint on how to solve the task,

550
00:26:17,760 --> 00:26:20,430
and that is something like a
natural language paragraph.

551
00:26:20,430 --> 00:26:24,660
And then we embed that
and use that as the query

552
00:26:24,660 --> 00:26:27,930
into the vector database,
and we retrieve the skill

553
00:26:27,930 --> 00:26:30,590
from the library.

554
00:26:30,590 --> 00:26:33,290
So you can think of it as
a kind of in-context replay

555
00:26:33,290 --> 00:26:37,720
buffer in the reinforcement
learning literature.

556
00:26:37,720 --> 00:26:40,950
And now, moving on
to the third part.

557
00:26:40,950 --> 00:26:44,070
We have another GPT-4 that
proposes a task to do,

558
00:26:44,070 --> 00:26:47,550
given its own capabilities
at the moment.

559
00:26:47,550 --> 00:26:50,460
And here, we give GPT-4
a very high-level kind

560
00:26:50,460 --> 00:26:52,740
of unsupervised
objective, that is,

561
00:26:52,740 --> 00:26:56,010
to obtain as many unique
items as possible.

562
00:26:56,010 --> 00:26:57,690
That is our
high-level directive.

563
00:26:57,690 --> 00:26:59,610
And then GPT-4
takes this directive

564
00:26:59,610 --> 00:27:04,710
and implements a curriculum of
progressively harder challenges

565
00:27:04,710 --> 00:27:06,870
and more novel
challenges to solve.

566
00:27:06,870 --> 00:27:11,850
So it's kind of like
curiosity exploration, where

567
00:27:11,850 --> 00:27:14,220
it is called novelty search
in the prior literature

568
00:27:14,220 --> 00:27:16,360
but implemented
purely in context.

569
00:27:16,360 --> 00:27:20,032
Yeah, if you are listening, then
assume the next example is fun.

570
00:27:20,032 --> 00:27:21,900
[LAUGHING]

571
00:27:21,900 --> 00:27:24,660
Let's go through this
example together, right?

572
00:27:24,660 --> 00:27:27,120
Just to show you
how Voyager works,

573
00:27:27,120 --> 00:27:30,420
the whole complicated data
flow that I just showed.

574
00:27:30,420 --> 00:27:33,480
So, the agent finds
itself hungry.

575
00:27:33,480 --> 00:27:35,580
It only has one out
of 20 hunger bar.

576
00:27:35,580 --> 00:27:39,100
So GPT-4 knows that it
needs to find food ASAP.

577
00:27:39,100 --> 00:27:43,960
And then it senses there are
four entities nearby, a cat,

578
00:27:43,960 --> 00:27:46,350
a villager, a pig,
and some wheat seeds.

579
00:27:46,350 --> 00:27:49,160
And now GPT-4 starts a
self-reflection, right?

580
00:27:49,160 --> 00:27:52,070
Like, do I kill the cat and
villager to get some meat?

581
00:27:52,070 --> 00:27:54,160
That sounds horrible.

582
00:27:54,160 --> 00:27:55,870
How about the wheat seeds?

583
00:27:55,870 --> 00:27:57,610
I can use the seeds
to grow a farm,

584
00:27:57,610 --> 00:27:59,500
but that's going to
take a very long time

585
00:27:59,500 --> 00:28:01,410
until I can generate some food.

586
00:28:01,410 --> 00:28:02,870
So, sorry, Piggy.

587
00:28:02,870 --> 00:28:04,185
You are the one being chosen.

588
00:28:04,185 --> 00:28:05,050
[LAUGHTER]

589
00:28:05,050 --> 00:28:09,160
So GPT-4 looks at the inventory,
which is the agent state.

590
00:28:09,160 --> 00:28:11,800
There is a piece of
iron in inventory.

591
00:28:11,800 --> 00:28:13,000
So it recalls--

592
00:28:13,000 --> 00:28:16,060
Voyager recalls a skill
from the library, that

593
00:28:16,060 --> 00:28:19,060
is to craft an iron sword
and then use that skill

594
00:28:19,060 --> 00:28:22,220
to start pursuing, to
start learning a new skill,

595
00:28:22,220 --> 00:28:23,700
and that is hunt pig.

596
00:28:23,700 --> 00:28:27,910
And once the hunt-pig
routine is successful,

597
00:28:27,910 --> 00:28:30,390
GPT-4 saves it to
the skill library.

598
00:28:30,390 --> 00:28:32,930
That's roughly how it works.

599
00:28:32,930 --> 00:28:33,430
Yeah.

600
00:28:33,430 --> 00:28:34,960
And putting all
of these together,

601
00:28:34,960 --> 00:28:38,170
we have this iterative prompting
mechanism, the skill library,

602
00:28:38,170 --> 00:28:40,180
and an automatic curriculum.

603
00:28:40,180 --> 00:28:43,320
And all of these
combine is Voyager's

604
00:28:43,320 --> 00:28:47,400
no-gradient architecture, where
we don't train any new models

605
00:28:47,400 --> 00:28:52,740
or fine-tune any parameters, and
allows Voyager to self-bootstrap

606
00:28:52,740 --> 00:28:55,290
on top of GPT-4
even though we are

607
00:28:55,290 --> 00:28:57,650
treating the underlying
language model as a black box.

608
00:28:57,650 --> 00:29:00,640

609
00:29:00,640 --> 00:29:04,590
It looks like my example worked,
and they started to listen.

610
00:29:04,590 --> 00:29:05,920
[LAUGHS]

611
00:29:05,920 --> 00:29:08,690
Yeah.

612
00:29:08,690 --> 00:29:11,570
So yeah, these are the
tasks that Voyager picked up

613
00:29:11,570 --> 00:29:12,510
along the way.

614
00:29:12,510 --> 00:29:14,670
And we didn't
pre-program any of these.

615
00:29:14,670 --> 00:29:16,610
It's all Voyager's idea.

616
00:29:16,610 --> 00:29:18,830
The agent is kind of
forever curious and also

617
00:29:18,830 --> 00:29:23,780
forever pursuing new
adventures just by itself.

618
00:29:23,780 --> 00:29:27,530
So to quickly show some
quantitative results,

619
00:29:27,530 --> 00:29:30,080
here we have a
learning curve where

620
00:29:30,080 --> 00:29:33,200
the x-axis is the number
of prompting iterations,

621
00:29:33,200 --> 00:29:36,080
and the y-axis is the
number of unique items

622
00:29:36,080 --> 00:29:39,980
that Voyager discovered as
it's exploring in environment.

623
00:29:39,980 --> 00:29:47,390
And these two curves are
baselines, ReAct and Reflection.

624
00:29:47,390 --> 00:29:50,910
And this is AutoGPT, which is
like a popular software repo.

625
00:29:50,910 --> 00:29:53,060
Basically, you can
think of it as combining

626
00:29:53,060 --> 00:29:55,820
ReAct and a task
planner that decomposes

627
00:29:55,820 --> 00:29:58,130
an objective into subgoals.

628
00:29:58,130 --> 00:29:59,870
And this is Voyager.

629
00:29:59,870 --> 00:30:02,360
We're able to obtain
three times more novel

630
00:30:02,360 --> 00:30:06,350
items than the prior methods
and also unlock the entire tech

631
00:30:06,350 --> 00:30:08,720
tree significantly faster.

632
00:30:08,720 --> 00:30:11,690
And if you take away
the scale library,

633
00:30:11,690 --> 00:30:13,500
you see that Voyager
really suffers.

634
00:30:13,500 --> 00:30:16,490
The performance takes a
hit because every time

635
00:30:16,490 --> 00:30:18,530
it needs to kind of
repeat and relearn

636
00:30:18,530 --> 00:30:20,450
every skill from
scratch, and it starts

637
00:30:20,450 --> 00:30:23,400
to make a lot more
mistakes, and that really

638
00:30:23,400 --> 00:30:26,510
degrades the exploration.

639
00:30:26,510 --> 00:30:30,750
Here, these two are the bird-eye
views of the Minecraft map.

640
00:30:30,750 --> 00:30:34,010
And these circles are
what the prior methods

641
00:30:34,010 --> 00:30:38,960
are able to explore given the
same prompting iteration budget.

642
00:30:38,960 --> 00:30:41,990
And we see that they tend
to get stuck in local areas

643
00:30:41,990 --> 00:30:44,750
and kind of fail
to explore more.

644
00:30:44,750 --> 00:30:46,400
But the Voyager is
able to navigate

645
00:30:46,400 --> 00:30:52,420
distances at least two times
as much as the prior works.

646
00:30:52,420 --> 00:30:55,210
So it's able to visit
a lot more places

647
00:30:55,210 --> 00:30:58,210
because to satisfy this
high-level directive

648
00:30:58,210 --> 00:31:00,760
of obtaining as many
unique items as possible,

649
00:31:00,760 --> 00:31:02,280
you've got to travel, right?

650
00:31:02,280 --> 00:31:04,030
If you stay at one
place, you will quickly

651
00:31:04,030 --> 00:31:06,260
exhaust the interesting
things to do.

652
00:31:06,260 --> 00:31:08,480
And Voyager travels
a lot, so that's

653
00:31:08,480 --> 00:31:11,640
how we came up with the name.

654
00:31:11,640 --> 00:31:15,660
So finally, one limitation is
that Voyager does not currently

655
00:31:15,660 --> 00:31:19,020
support visual perception
because the GPT-4 that we

656
00:31:19,020 --> 00:31:21,330
used back then was text only.

657
00:31:21,330 --> 00:31:23,130
But there's nothing
stopping Voyager

658
00:31:23,130 --> 00:31:27,160
from adopting like multimodal
language models in the future.

659
00:31:27,160 --> 00:31:29,400
So here we have a little
proof-of-concept demo

660
00:31:29,400 --> 00:31:32,820
where we ask a human to
basically function as the image

661
00:31:32,820 --> 00:31:36,150
captioner, and the
human will tell Voyager

662
00:31:36,150 --> 00:31:38,503
that as you're building
these houses, what are

663
00:31:38,503 --> 00:31:39,670
the things that are missing?

664
00:31:39,670 --> 00:31:42,180
Like, you place a
door incorrectly.

665
00:31:42,180 --> 00:31:45,010
Like, the roof is also
not done correctly.

666
00:31:45,010 --> 00:31:48,450
So the human is acting as a
critic module of the Voyager

667
00:31:48,450 --> 00:31:49,140
stack.

668
00:31:49,140 --> 00:31:52,050
And we see that with
some of that help,

669
00:31:52,050 --> 00:31:55,350
Voyager is able to build a
farm house and a nether portal,

670
00:31:55,350 --> 00:31:58,770
but it has a hard time
understanding 3D spatial

671
00:31:58,770 --> 00:32:01,625
coordinates just by itself
in a textual domain.

672
00:32:01,625 --> 00:32:04,680

673
00:32:04,680 --> 00:32:08,280
Now, after doing Voyager,
we're considering,

674
00:32:08,280 --> 00:32:12,450
like, where else can we
apply this idea of coding

675
00:32:12,450 --> 00:32:15,330
in an embodied environment,
observe the feedback,

676
00:32:15,330 --> 00:32:18,540
and iteratively
refine the program?

677
00:32:18,540 --> 00:32:22,800
So we came to realize that
physics simulations themselves

678
00:32:22,800 --> 00:32:24,450
are also just Python code.

679
00:32:24,450 --> 00:32:27,660
So why not apply some of
the principles from Voyager

680
00:32:27,660 --> 00:32:31,250
and do something
in another domain?

681
00:32:31,250 --> 00:32:34,100
What if you apply Voyager in a
space of this physics simulator

682
00:32:34,100 --> 00:32:35,070
API?

683
00:32:35,070 --> 00:32:38,870
And this is Eureka, which
my team announced just

684
00:32:38,870 --> 00:32:39,930
like three days ago.

685
00:32:39,930 --> 00:32:42,080
Fresh out of the oven.

686
00:32:42,080 --> 00:32:44,900
It is an open-ended
agent that designs

687
00:32:44,900 --> 00:32:49,280
reward functions for robot
dexterity at superhuman level.

688
00:32:49,280 --> 00:32:52,250
And it turns out that GPT-4
plus reinforcement learning

689
00:32:52,250 --> 00:32:56,030
can spin a pen much
better than I do.

690
00:32:56,030 --> 00:32:59,870
I gave up on this task a long
time ago, from childhood.

691
00:32:59,870 --> 00:33:03,300
It's so hard for me.

692
00:33:03,300 --> 00:33:07,020
So Eureka's idea is very
simple and intuitive.

693
00:33:07,020 --> 00:33:10,740
GPT-4 generates a bunch of
possible reward function

694
00:33:10,740 --> 00:33:12,630
candidates
implemented in Python,

695
00:33:12,630 --> 00:33:16,350
and then you just do a
full reinforcement learning

696
00:33:16,350 --> 00:33:21,390
training loop for each candidate
in a GPU accelerator simulator.

697
00:33:21,390 --> 00:33:23,460
And you get a
performance metric,

698
00:33:23,460 --> 00:33:27,000
and you take the best candidates
and feedback to GPT-4,

699
00:33:27,000 --> 00:33:30,450
and it samples the next
proposals of the candidates

700
00:33:30,450 --> 00:33:33,240
and keeps improving the whole
population of the reward

701
00:33:33,240 --> 00:33:34,200
functions.

702
00:33:34,200 --> 00:33:35,820
That's the whole idea.

703
00:33:35,820 --> 00:33:40,130
It's kind of like an
in-context evolutionary search.

704
00:33:40,130 --> 00:33:42,230
So here's the initial
reward generation,

705
00:33:42,230 --> 00:33:47,000
where Eureka takes as context
the environment code of NVIDIA's

706
00:33:47,000 --> 00:33:50,240
Isaac Sim, and a
task description,

707
00:33:50,240 --> 00:33:54,220
and samples the initial reward
function implementation.

708
00:33:54,220 --> 00:33:56,920
So we found that the simulator
called itself is actually

709
00:33:56,920 --> 00:34:00,100
a very good reference manual
because it tells Eureka what

710
00:34:00,100 --> 00:34:02,230
are the variables you can use.

711
00:34:02,230 --> 00:34:03,940
Like, you know,
the hand positions.

712
00:34:03,940 --> 00:34:06,520
Like, here, the fingertip
position, the fingertip save,

713
00:34:06,520 --> 00:34:09,460
the rotation angular
velocity, et cetera.

714
00:34:09,460 --> 00:34:11,800
So, you know all
of these variables

715
00:34:11,800 --> 00:34:14,620
from the simulator
code, and you know how

716
00:34:14,620 --> 00:34:16,270
they interact with each other.

717
00:34:16,270 --> 00:34:21,250
So that serves as a very
good in-context instruction.

718
00:34:21,250 --> 00:34:23,199
So Eureka doesn't
need to reference

719
00:34:23,199 --> 00:34:26,409
any human-written
reward functions.

720
00:34:26,409 --> 00:34:28,929
And then, once you have
the generated reward,

721
00:34:28,929 --> 00:34:31,540
you plug it into any
reinforcement learning algorithm

722
00:34:31,540 --> 00:34:33,510
and just train it to completion.

723
00:34:33,510 --> 00:34:37,840
So this step is typically
very costly and very slow

724
00:34:37,840 --> 00:34:39,880
because reinforcement
learning itself is slow.

725
00:34:39,880 --> 00:34:44,530
And we were only able to scale
up Eureka because of [INAUDIBLE]

726
00:34:44,530 --> 00:34:49,179
which runs 1,000 simulated
environment copies on a single

727
00:34:49,179 --> 00:34:49,969
GPU.

728
00:34:49,969 --> 00:34:53,800
So basically, you can think
of it as speeding up reality

729
00:34:53,800 --> 00:34:56,565
by 1,000x.

730
00:34:56,565 --> 00:35:00,010
And then, after training, you
will get the performance metrics

731
00:35:00,010 --> 00:35:02,180
back on each reward component.

732
00:35:02,180 --> 00:35:04,840
And as we saw from
Voyager, GPT-4

733
00:35:04,840 --> 00:35:07,040
is very good at self-reflection.

734
00:35:07,040 --> 00:35:09,500
So we leverage that capability.

735
00:35:09,500 --> 00:35:15,640
And there's a software
trial reminding

736
00:35:15,640 --> 00:35:18,510
you to activate a license.

737
00:35:18,510 --> 00:35:19,010
Yeah.

738
00:35:19,010 --> 00:35:24,200
So Voyager reflects on it
and then proposes mutations

739
00:35:24,200 --> 00:35:26,450
on the code.

740
00:35:26,450 --> 00:35:29,990
So here, the mutations we
found can be very diverse,

741
00:35:29,990 --> 00:35:32,180
ranging from something as
simple as just changing

742
00:35:32,180 --> 00:35:36,200
a hyperparameter in the reward
function waiting all the way

743
00:35:36,200 --> 00:35:39,110
to adding completely novel
components to the reward

744
00:35:39,110 --> 00:35:40,920
function.

745
00:35:40,920 --> 00:35:44,280
And in our experiments,
Eureka turns out

746
00:35:44,280 --> 00:35:48,390
to be a superhuman reward
engineer, actually outperforming

747
00:35:48,390 --> 00:35:50,430
some of the
functions implemented

748
00:35:50,430 --> 00:35:56,750
by the expert human engineers
on NVIDIA's Isaac Sim team.

749
00:35:56,750 --> 00:35:58,820
So here are some
more demos of how

750
00:35:58,820 --> 00:36:02,360
Eureka is able to write
very complex rewards that

751
00:36:02,360 --> 00:36:05,640
lead to these extremely
dexterous behaviors.

752
00:36:05,640 --> 00:36:09,380
And we can actually train the
robot hand to rotate pens.

753
00:36:09,380 --> 00:36:11,120
Not just, you know,
in one direction,

754
00:36:11,120 --> 00:36:15,380
but in different directions
along different pseudo-axis.

755
00:36:15,380 --> 00:36:17,690
I think one major
contribution of Eureka,

756
00:36:17,690 --> 00:36:20,000
different from
Voyager, is to bridge

757
00:36:20,000 --> 00:36:23,720
the gap between high-level
reasoning and low-level motor

758
00:36:23,720 --> 00:36:24,680
controls.

759
00:36:24,680 --> 00:36:26,960
So Eureka introduces
a new paradigm

760
00:36:26,960 --> 00:36:30,290
that I'm calling hybrid
gradient architecture.

761
00:36:30,290 --> 00:36:32,730
So recall, Voyager is a
no-gradient architecture.

762
00:36:32,730 --> 00:36:35,670
We don't touch anything,
and we don't train anything.

763
00:36:35,670 --> 00:36:40,190
But Eureka is a hybrid gradient
where a black box inference-only

764
00:36:40,190 --> 00:36:44,900
language model instructs a white
box learnable neural network.

765
00:36:44,900 --> 00:36:47,130
So you can think
of it as two loops.

766
00:36:47,130 --> 00:36:47,630
Right?

767
00:36:47,630 --> 00:36:49,560
The outer loop is
greater than 3,

768
00:36:49,560 --> 00:36:54,200
and it's driven by GPT-4 kind of
selecting the reward functions.

769
00:36:54,200 --> 00:36:56,220
And the inner loop
is gradient-based.

770
00:36:56,220 --> 00:36:58,760
You train it like a full
reinforcement learning episode

771
00:36:58,760 --> 00:37:01,970
from it to achieve
extreme dexterity

772
00:37:01,970 --> 00:37:03,950
using a specialized like--

773
00:37:03,950 --> 00:37:07,160
by training a special
neural network controller.

774
00:37:07,160 --> 00:37:09,920
And you must have both
loops to succeed to deliver

775
00:37:09,920 --> 00:37:11,420
this kind of dexterity.

776
00:37:11,420 --> 00:37:15,510
And I think it will be a very
useful paradigm for training

777
00:37:15,510 --> 00:37:18,730
robot agents in the future.

778
00:37:18,730 --> 00:37:23,530
So, you know, these days,
when I go on Twitter, or X,

779
00:37:23,530 --> 00:37:27,220
I see AI conquering
new lands every week--

780
00:37:27,220 --> 00:37:30,220
you know, chat, image
generation, and music.

781
00:37:30,220 --> 00:37:32,800
They're all very
well within reach.

782
00:37:32,800 --> 00:37:35,410
But MineDojo,
Voyager, and Eureka,

783
00:37:35,410 --> 00:37:38,710
these are just scratching the
surface of open-ended generalist

784
00:37:38,710 --> 00:37:40,600
agents.

785
00:37:40,600 --> 00:37:43,750
And looking forward, I want
to share two key research

786
00:37:43,750 --> 00:37:47,530
directions that I personally
find extremely promising,

787
00:37:47,530 --> 00:37:49,960
and I'm also working
on it myself.

788
00:37:49,960 --> 00:37:52,180
The first is a
continuation of MineCLIP,

789
00:37:52,180 --> 00:37:55,480
basically, how to develop
methods that learn

790
00:37:55,480 --> 00:37:57,360
from internet-scale videos.

791
00:37:57,360 --> 00:38:00,760
And the second is multimodal
foundation models now

792
00:38:00,760 --> 00:38:05,230
that GPT-4V is coming, but it
is just the beginning of an era.

793
00:38:05,230 --> 00:38:09,070
And I think it's important
to have all the modalities

794
00:38:09,070 --> 00:38:12,430
in a single foundation model.

795
00:38:12,430 --> 00:38:14,820
So first, about videos.

796
00:38:14,820 --> 00:38:17,370
We all know that videos
are abundant, right?

797
00:38:17,370 --> 00:38:21,450
Like, so many data on
YouTube, way too many

798
00:38:21,450 --> 00:38:24,180
for our limited GPUs to process.

799
00:38:24,180 --> 00:38:26,580
They're extremely
useful to train

800
00:38:26,580 --> 00:38:30,390
models that not only have
dynamic perception and intuitive

801
00:38:30,390 --> 00:38:34,500
physics but also capture the
complexity of human creativity

802
00:38:34,500 --> 00:38:36,090
and human behaviors.

803
00:38:36,090 --> 00:38:40,860
It's all good, except that
when you are using video

804
00:38:40,860 --> 00:38:42,930
to pre-train embodied
agents, there

805
00:38:42,930 --> 00:38:44,440
is huge distribution shift.

806
00:38:44,440 --> 00:38:46,285
You also don't get
the action labels,

807
00:38:46,285 --> 00:38:47,910
and you don't get
any of the groundings

808
00:38:47,910 --> 00:38:50,760
because you're a
passive observer.

809
00:38:50,760 --> 00:38:52,350
So I think here
is a demonstration

810
00:38:52,350 --> 00:38:54,180
of why learning from
video is hard, even

811
00:38:54,180 --> 00:38:56,950
for natural intelligence.

812
00:38:56,950 --> 00:39:01,000
So little cat is seeing
boxers shaking their head

813
00:39:01,000 --> 00:39:05,231
and it thinks maybe shaking head
is the best way to do fighting.

814
00:39:05,231 --> 00:39:07,440
[LAUGHING]

815
00:39:07,440 --> 00:39:08,346
Right?

816
00:39:08,346 --> 00:39:10,370
This is why learning
from video is hard.

817
00:39:10,370 --> 00:39:14,020
[LAUGHING]

818
00:39:14,020 --> 00:39:15,940
You have no idea, like, why--

819
00:39:15,940 --> 00:39:18,070
[LAUGHS]

820
00:39:18,070 --> 00:39:18,880
This is too good.

821
00:39:18,880 --> 00:39:20,030
Let's play this again.

822
00:39:20,030 --> 00:39:22,990
You have no idea why Tyson
is doing this, right?

823
00:39:22,990 --> 00:39:24,230
Like the cat has no idea.

824
00:39:24,230 --> 00:39:28,245
And then it associates this with
just the wrong kind of policy.

825
00:39:28,245 --> 00:39:30,520
[LAUGHING]

826
00:39:30,520 --> 00:39:33,460
But for sure, you know, it
doesn't help the fighting,

827
00:39:33,460 --> 00:39:35,990
but it definitely boosts
the cat's confidence.

828
00:39:35,990 --> 00:39:38,950
[LAUGHING]

829
00:39:38,950 --> 00:39:42,260
That's why learning
from video is hard.

830
00:39:42,260 --> 00:39:45,080
Now, I want to point out
a few of latest research

831
00:39:45,080 --> 00:39:49,070
in how to leverage so much
video for generalist agents.

832
00:39:49,070 --> 00:39:51,210
There are a couple
of approaches.

833
00:39:51,210 --> 00:39:52,730
The first is the simplest.

834
00:39:52,730 --> 00:39:54,740
Just learn kind of
a visual feature

835
00:39:54,740 --> 00:39:56,740
extractor from the videos.

836
00:39:56,740 --> 00:40:00,950
So this is R3M from
Chelsea's group at Stanford.

837
00:40:00,950 --> 00:40:03,500
And this model is still an
image-level representation,

838
00:40:03,500 --> 00:40:05,870
just that it uses
a video-level loss

839
00:40:05,870 --> 00:40:08,210
function to train
more specifically

840
00:40:08,210 --> 00:40:09,830
time-contrastive learning.

841
00:40:09,830 --> 00:40:13,610
And after that, you can use
this as an image backbone

842
00:40:13,610 --> 00:40:15,590
for any agent,
but you still need

843
00:40:15,590 --> 00:40:18,440
to fine-tune using
domain-specific data

844
00:40:18,440 --> 00:40:20,850
for the agent.

845
00:40:20,850 --> 00:40:24,360
The second path is to learn
reward functions from video,

846
00:40:24,360 --> 00:40:29,310
and MineCLIP is one model
under this category.

847
00:40:29,310 --> 00:40:32,040
It uses a contrastive objective
between the transcript

848
00:40:32,040 --> 00:40:32,860
and video.

849
00:40:32,860 --> 00:40:36,270
And here, this work,
VIP, is another way

850
00:40:36,270 --> 00:40:38,520
to learn a
similarity-based reward

851
00:40:38,520 --> 00:40:41,520
for goal-conditioned
tasks in the image space.

852
00:40:41,520 --> 00:40:44,130
So this work, VIP, is led
by [AUDIO OUT],, who was also

853
00:40:44,130 --> 00:40:48,090
the first author of Eureka, and
Eureka is his internship project

854
00:40:48,090 --> 00:40:50,180
with me.

855
00:40:50,180 --> 00:40:52,340
And the third idea
is very interesting.

856
00:40:52,340 --> 00:40:56,330
Can we directly do imitation
learning from video,

857
00:40:56,330 --> 00:40:59,630
but better than the
cat that we just saw?

858
00:40:59,630 --> 00:41:01,910
So we just said,
you know, the videos

859
00:41:01,910 --> 00:41:04,280
don't have the actions, right?

860
00:41:04,280 --> 00:41:07,430
We need to find some ways
to pseudo-label the actions.

861
00:41:07,430 --> 00:41:10,670
And this is video pre-training
of GPT from OpenAI

862
00:41:10,670 --> 00:41:14,480
last year to solve long-range
tasks in Minecraft.

863
00:41:14,480 --> 00:41:18,200
And here, the pipeline
works like this.

864
00:41:18,200 --> 00:41:21,870
Basically, you use a keyboard
and a mouse, action space.

865
00:41:21,870 --> 00:41:25,790
So you can align this action
space with the human actions.

866
00:41:25,790 --> 00:41:29,660
And OpenAI hires a bunch
of Minecraft players

867
00:41:29,660 --> 00:41:31,800
and actually collect
data in-house.

868
00:41:31,800 --> 00:41:34,910
So they record the episodes
done by those gamers.

869
00:41:34,910 --> 00:41:39,770
And now you have a data set of
video and action pairs, right?

870
00:41:39,770 --> 00:41:42,980
And you train something called
an inverse dynamics model, which

871
00:41:42,980 --> 00:41:46,100
is to take the observation
and then predict

872
00:41:46,100 --> 00:41:49,140
the actions that caused
the observation to change.

873
00:41:49,140 --> 00:41:50,910
So that's the inverse
dynamics model,

874
00:41:50,910 --> 00:41:54,510
and that becomes
a labeler that you

875
00:41:54,510 --> 00:41:57,840
can apply to in-the-wild
YouTube videos that

876
00:41:57,840 --> 00:41:59,180
don't have the actions.

877
00:41:59,180 --> 00:42:03,660
So you apply the IDM to 70K
hours of in-the-wild YouTube

878
00:42:03,660 --> 00:42:06,900
videos, and you will get these
pseudo-actions that are not

879
00:42:06,900 --> 00:42:10,050
always correct but also
way better than random.

880
00:42:10,050 --> 00:42:12,570
And then you train
imitation learning on top

881
00:42:12,570 --> 00:42:14,340
of this augmented data set.

882
00:42:14,340 --> 00:42:18,270
And in this way, OpenAI
is able to greatly expand

883
00:42:18,270 --> 00:42:22,590
the data because the original
data collected from the humans

884
00:42:22,590 --> 00:42:25,320
are high quality, but
they're extremely expensive.

885
00:42:25,320 --> 00:42:27,540
While in-the-wild YouTube
videos are very cheap,

886
00:42:27,540 --> 00:42:29,580
but you don't have the
actions, so they kind of

887
00:42:29,580 --> 00:42:32,670
solved and got the
best of both worlds.

888
00:42:32,670 --> 00:42:37,470
But still, you know, it's really
expensive to hire these humans.

889
00:42:37,470 --> 00:42:40,440
Now, what's beyond
the videos, right?

890
00:42:40,440 --> 00:42:43,080
I'm a firm believer that
multimodal models will be

891
00:42:43,080 --> 00:42:47,520
the future, and I see text as a
very lossy kind of 1D projection

892
00:42:47,520 --> 00:42:49,040
of our physical world.

893
00:42:49,040 --> 00:42:52,530
So it's essential to include
the other sensory modalities

894
00:42:52,530 --> 00:42:54,990
to provide a full,
embodied experience.

895
00:42:54,990 --> 00:42:57,480
And in the context
of embodied agents,

896
00:42:57,480 --> 00:43:01,500
I think the input will be a
mixture of text, images, videos,

897
00:43:01,500 --> 00:43:06,390
and even audio in the future,
and the output will be actions.

898
00:43:06,390 --> 00:43:10,800
So here's a very early example
of a multimodal language

899
00:43:10,800 --> 00:43:12,600
model for robot learning.

900
00:43:12,600 --> 00:43:15,060
So let's imagine
a household robot.

901
00:43:15,060 --> 00:43:18,960
We can ask the robot to bring us
a cup of tea from the kitchen.

902
00:43:18,960 --> 00:43:21,060
But if we want to
be more specific,

903
00:43:21,060 --> 00:43:22,710
I want this particular cup.

904
00:43:22,710 --> 00:43:23,830
That is my favorite cup.

905
00:43:23,830 --> 00:43:26,210
So show me this image.

906
00:43:26,210 --> 00:43:31,490
And we also provide a video demo
of how we want to mop the floor

907
00:43:31,490 --> 00:43:36,160
and ask the robot to imitate
the similar motion in context.

908
00:43:36,160 --> 00:43:38,050
And when the robot sees
an unfamiliar object

909
00:43:38,050 --> 00:43:41,050
like a sweeper, we can explain
it by providing an image

910
00:43:41,050 --> 00:43:42,500
and showing, this is a sweeper.

911
00:43:42,500 --> 00:43:45,440
Now, go ahead and do
something with the tool.

912
00:43:45,440 --> 00:43:47,170
And finally, to
ensure safety, we

913
00:43:47,170 --> 00:43:48,880
can say, take a
picture of that room,

914
00:43:48,880 --> 00:43:51,470
and just do not enter that room.

915
00:43:51,470 --> 00:43:55,340
To achieve this,
back last year, we

916
00:43:55,340 --> 00:43:58,610
proposed a model called VIMA,
which stands for visual motor

917
00:43:58,610 --> 00:43:59,540
attention.

918
00:43:59,540 --> 00:44:02,240
And in this work, we introduced
a concept called multimodal

919
00:44:02,240 --> 00:44:06,800
prompting, where the prompt can
be a mixture of text, image,

920
00:44:06,800 --> 00:44:07,940
and videos.

921
00:44:07,940 --> 00:44:11,960
And this provides a very
expressive API that just unifies

922
00:44:11,960 --> 00:44:16,010
a bunch of different robot tasks
that otherwise would require

923
00:44:16,010 --> 00:44:18,320
a very different pipeline
or specialized models

924
00:44:18,320 --> 00:44:20,330
to solve in prior literature.

925
00:44:20,330 --> 00:44:25,040
And VIMA simply
tokenizes everything,

926
00:44:25,040 --> 00:44:27,950
converting image and text
into sequences of tokens

927
00:44:27,950 --> 00:44:29,690
and training a
transformer on top

928
00:44:29,690 --> 00:44:33,770
to output the robot arm
actions autoregressively

929
00:44:33,770 --> 00:44:37,240
one step at a time
during inference time.

930
00:44:37,240 --> 00:44:40,210
So just to look at some
of the examples here.

931
00:44:40,210 --> 00:44:43,640
Like, this prompt rearrange
objects to match the scene.

932
00:44:43,640 --> 00:44:45,310
It's a classical task
called visual goal

933
00:44:45,310 --> 00:44:48,730
reaching that has a big
body of prior works on it.

934
00:44:48,730 --> 00:44:53,920
And that's how our robot
does it, given this prompt.

935
00:44:53,920 --> 00:44:57,550
And we can also give it
novel concepts in context.

936
00:44:57,550 --> 00:44:58,730
Like, this is a blicket.

937
00:44:58,730 --> 00:44:59,620
This is a wug.

938
00:44:59,620 --> 00:45:03,460
Now, put the wug into a blicket,
and both words are nonsensical.

939
00:45:03,460 --> 00:45:05,110
So it's not in
the training data,

940
00:45:05,110 --> 00:45:09,280
but VIMA is able to generalize
zero-shot and follow the motion

941
00:45:09,280 --> 00:45:11,140
to manipulate this object.

942
00:45:11,140 --> 00:45:14,140
So the bot understands what
we want and then follow

943
00:45:14,140 --> 00:45:15,290
this trajectory.

944
00:45:15,290 --> 00:45:18,080
And finally, we can give
it a more complex prompt.

945
00:45:18,080 --> 00:45:20,090
Like, these are the
safety constraints.

946
00:45:20,090 --> 00:45:23,560
Sweep the box into this, but
without exceeding that line.

947
00:45:23,560 --> 00:45:27,850
And we do this using the
interleaving image and text

948
00:45:27,850 --> 00:45:30,480
tokens.

949
00:45:30,480 --> 00:45:34,230
And recently, Google Brain
Robotics followed up after VIMA

950
00:45:34,230 --> 00:45:38,910
with RT-1 and RT-2, robot
transformer 1 and 2.

951
00:45:38,910 --> 00:45:40,830
And RT-2 is using
a similar recipe

952
00:45:40,830 --> 00:45:43,500
as I described,
where they first kind

953
00:45:43,500 --> 00:45:46,410
of pre-train on
internet-scale data

954
00:45:46,410 --> 00:45:49,740
and then fine-tune with some
human-collected demonstrations

955
00:45:49,740 --> 00:45:51,300
on the Google robots.

956
00:45:51,300 --> 00:45:54,780
And RoboCat from DeepMind
is another interesting work.

957
00:45:54,780 --> 00:45:56,610
They train a single,
unified policy

958
00:45:56,610 --> 00:46:00,540
that works not just
on a single robot

959
00:46:00,540 --> 00:46:03,780
but actually across different
embodiments, different robot

960
00:46:03,780 --> 00:46:07,190
forms, and even generalize
to a new hardware.

961
00:46:07,190 --> 00:46:10,490
So I think this is like a
higher form of multimodal agent

962
00:46:10,490 --> 00:46:13,670
where the physical form factor,
the morphology of the agent

963
00:46:13,670 --> 00:46:17,270
itself, is another modality.

964
00:46:17,270 --> 00:46:21,640
So that concludes our
Looking Forward section.

965
00:46:21,640 --> 00:46:25,270
And lastly, I want to put
all the links together

966
00:46:25,270 --> 00:46:27,170
of the works I described.

967
00:46:27,170 --> 00:46:29,000
So this is minedojo.org.

968
00:46:29,000 --> 00:46:31,360
We have open-source everything.

969
00:46:31,360 --> 00:46:34,700
Well, for all the projects, we
are big fans of open source.

970
00:46:34,700 --> 00:46:36,610
We open source as
much as we can,

971
00:46:36,610 --> 00:46:41,920
including like the model code,
checkpoints, simulator code,

972
00:46:41,920 --> 00:46:44,520
and training data.

973
00:46:44,520 --> 00:46:47,490
And this is
voyager.minedojo.org.

974
00:46:47,490 --> 00:46:49,530
This is Eureka.

975
00:46:49,530 --> 00:46:53,250
And this is VIMA.

976
00:46:53,250 --> 00:46:54,870
And one more thing, right?

977
00:46:54,870 --> 00:46:58,590
If you just want an excuse
to play Minecraft at work,

978
00:46:58,590 --> 00:47:00,900
then MineDojo is perfect
for you because you

979
00:47:00,900 --> 00:47:02,760
are collecting
human demonstration

980
00:47:02,760 --> 00:47:04,020
to train generalist agent.

981
00:47:04,020 --> 00:47:06,900
And if there's one thing that
you take away from this talk,

982
00:47:06,900 --> 00:47:09,880
it should be this slide.

983
00:47:09,880 --> 00:47:12,250
And lastly, I just want
to remind all of us.

984
00:47:12,250 --> 00:47:14,590
You know, despite all
the progress I've shown,

985
00:47:14,590 --> 00:47:18,940
what we can do is still very
far from human ingenuity

986
00:47:18,940 --> 00:47:20,710
as embodied agents.

987
00:47:20,710 --> 00:47:23,920
You know, these are the
videos from our data set

988
00:47:23,920 --> 00:47:26,950
of people doing like
decorating a winter wonderland

